The three factors in \eqref{eq:policy} are the three steps of the classical recipe: an anticausal whitening, the adapted projection, and a causal recolouring. The whitening would call for the future of the signal; the projection supplies instead its forecast (\S\ref{sec:meanrev}). Only the friction's factor whitens the signal, the signal's own spectral factor entering through the projection alone. Completing the square gives a second reading. Up to a constant the objective \eqref{eq:objective} equals $-\tfrac12\|x-N^{-1}\mu\|_N^2$ in the friction norm $\|y\|_N^2=\E\langle y,Ny\rangle$, so that $x^\star$ is the orthogonal projection, in that norm, of the anticipative optimum $N^{-1}\mu$ onto the adapted positions --- equivalently the causal Wiener--Kolmogorov estimate of the whitened return from the past.

